Yes. A police officer in uniform can seize the keys of a vehicle in certain circumstances. 

Under section 206 of the Motor Vehicles (Amendment) Act, 2019, a police officer or an authorized person may seize a driver's license if they reasonably believe that the driver has committed an offense under any of the sections 183, 184, 185, 189, 190, 194C, 194D, or 194E. 

Additionally, under section 130 of the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988, the driver of a motor vehicle in any public place must produce his license for examination. 

Another example is under section 213 of the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988, a police officer can search, inspect, and seize any documents or portions thereof as they reasonably believe are relevant for the purpose of this Act. This can happen without a warrant, but the gazetted officer must record in writing the grounds for not obtaining the warrant. 

The Haryana Motor Vehicle Rules, 1993, list the following officers who have the power to detain vehicles.

1. Transport Commissioner;
2. Additional Transport Commissioner;
3. Additional/Joint State Transport Controller;
4. Secretary, Regional Transport Authority;
5. Flying Squad Officers;
6. Deputy Transport Controller (Traffic);
7. General Manager, Haryana Roadways, Traffic Manager, Haryana Roadways, Station Supervisor/Chief Inspector, Haryana Roadways;
8. Transport Sub-Inspector; 

In general, a police officer has the power to seize a vehicle if it's believed that the driver has committed an offense under the laws of the country. This is because the police have the power to detain vehicles for the safety of the public.

According to the Central Motor Vehicles Rules, 1989, under Section 19, a police officer in uniform or an officer of the Motor Vehicles Department in uniform or any other officer authorized by the Government can demand the following documents from you in one single stop:

(i) Driving licence;
(ii) Certificate of registration;
(iii) Certificate of taxation; and
(iv) Certificate of insurance of the vehicle.

This is specified in Section 19 of the Central Motor Vehicles Rules, 1989, which states that a person driving a vehicle shall always carry these documents with them and shall produce them on demand by a police officer or an officer of the Motor Vehicles Department.
